insert into d (name, yr, txt) values
  ('Battle of Spion Kop', 1900, 'On 23-24 January 1900 a British force under General Warren climbed and captured the summit of Spion Kop, a hill overlooking the Tugela River, in another attempt to relieve the besieged town of Ladysmith in the Second Boer War. At dawn the mist lifted to show that they held only part of the summit, and Boer riflemen and guns on the surrounding heights shot them down in the shallow trenches they had dug in the rocky ground. The British withdrew after a day of heavy losses, several times those of the Boers. Among those present were the war correspondent Winston Churchill and the Indian stretcher-bearers led by Mohandas Gandhi, and the ''Kop'' terraces of football grounds are named after the hill.'),
  ('Battle of Port Arthur', 1904, 'On the night of 8 February 1904 Japanese destroyers made a surprise torpedo attack on the Russian Pacific Fleet anchored outside Port Arthur, in Manchuria, two days before war was formally declared. They damaged two battleships and a cruiser, and a gun duel with the Russian ships and shore batteries followed the next day. It began the Russo-Japanese War and showed Japan''s readiness to strike first, as it did again at Pearl Harbor in 1941. Port Arthur was then besieged by the Japanese army and surrendered in January 1905.'),
  ('Panama Canal construction', 1904, 'In 1904 the United States began building the Panama Canal, taking over a project that a French company had abandoned after its workers died in huge numbers, mostly of yellow fever and malaria. Dr William Gorgas''s campaign against mosquitoes, with drainage and fumigation, made the work possible, and the engineers John Stevens and George Goethals led some 40,000 workers who cut through the continental divide and built giant locks. The canal opened on 15 August 1914, saving ships the long voyage around South America, and it greatly increased American power and trade. The United States had backed Panama''s break from Colombia in 1903 to obtain the land, and it handed the canal over to Panama only at the end of 1999.'),
  ('Battle of Tsushima', 1905, 'On 27-28 May 1905 Admiral Togo''s Japanese fleet destroyed the Russian Baltic Fleet in the Tsushima Strait, between Korea and Japan, after the Russian ships had sailed some 18,000 nautical miles from the Baltic to reach the war zone. Almost the whole Russian force was sunk or captured, including most of its battleships, while Japan lost only three torpedo boats. It was the first great clash between modern steel battleship fleets, and the first in which wireless telegraphy played a major part. The defeat forced Russia toward peace, and Japan''s victory, the first by an Asian power over a European one in modern times, inspired nationalist movements across Asia.'),
  ('Iranian Constitutional Revolution', 1905, 'Between 1905 and 1911 merchants, clergy and intellectuals in Iran rose against the arbitrary rule of the Qajar shahs and the growing foreign control of the country''s economy. In August 1906 Shah Mozaffar ad-Din was forced to accept a constitution, and Iran''s first elected parliament, the Majlis, opened that October. His successor, Mohammad Ali Shah, bombarded the Majlis in 1908, but constitutionalists retook Tehran in 1909 and deposed him. The revolution brought Iran its first parliament and a written constitution, though Russian and British interference, and a Russian ultimatum in December 1911, ended this first experiment in parliamentary government.'),
  ('Russian Revolution of 1905', 1905, 'The Russian Revolution of 1905 began on 22 January, ''Bloody Sunday'', when troops fired on peaceful workers marching with a petition to the tsar in St Petersburg. It was followed by strikes, peasant revolts, a mutiny on the battleship Potemkin and a great general strike in October, as Russia was also reeling from its defeat by Japan. Nicholas II then issued the October Manifesto, promising civil liberties and an elected parliament, the Duma. The revolution was suppressed in the following months, and the tsar kept most of his power, but it was a rehearsal for the revolutions of 1917.'),
  ('Young Turk Revolution', 1908, 'In July 1908 officers of the Committee of Union and Progress, a secret society of ''Young Turks'', rebelled in Macedonia and forced Sultan Abdulhamid II to restore the constitution of 1876 and recall parliament. The revolution promised equal rights to all peoples of the empire and began the Second Constitutional Era. But the empire was soon hit by new crises, as Austria-Hungary annexed Bosnia and Bulgaria declared its independence in 1908. The Committee later turned toward Turkish nationalism, took full control in 1913, and led the empire into the First World War.'),
  ('Mexican Revolution', 1910, 'The Mexican Revolution began on 20 November 1910, when Francisco Madero called for an uprising against the dictator Porfirio Díaz, who had ruled Mexico for more than thirty years. It grew into a decade of civil war among many factions, among them the peasant leader Emiliano Zapata, the northern general Pancho Villa and the constitutionalists Venustiano Carranza and Álvaro Obregón, and it is thought to have cost over a million lives. The Constitution of 1917 gave Mexico land reform, workers'' rights and limits on the power of the Church. The revolution destroyed the old federal army and the landowning order, and it shaped Mexico''s politics for the rest of the century.'),
  ('Battle of Ciudad Juárez', 1911, 'In April and May 1911 the rebel army of Francisco Madero, with commanders Pascual Orozco and Pancho Villa, besieged Ciudad Juárez, on the border opposite El Paso, Texas, and captured it on 10 May after two days of fierce fighting against the federal garrison. It was the first great victory of the Mexican Revolution, and Americans watched the fighting from the rooftops across the river. The Treaty of Ciudad Juárez on 21 May arranged for President Porfirio Díaz to resign, and he left for exile in France. Madero became president, but he soon lost control of the forces he had unleashed.'),
  ('Xinhai Revolution', 1911, 'The Xinhai Revolution began with the Wuchang Uprising on 10 October 1911, when army units rebelled against the Qing dynasty in central China, and within weeks most provinces had declared independence. Sun Yat-sen, who had spent years raising money and support abroad for a republic, became provisional president in December. On 12 February 1912 the child emperor Puyi abdicated, ending more than two thousand years of imperial rule in China. The new republic was weak, however, and soon fell into the hands of warlords.'),
  ('First Balkan War', 1912, 'In October 1912 the Balkan League, made up of Bulgaria, Serbia, Greece and Montenegro, attacked the Ottoman Empire, and in a few weeks its armies won victories over the Ottoman forces, which were unprepared and short of supplies. The Ottomans lost almost all their European lands, including Macedonia, most of Thrace and Albania, and Albania declared independence. The victors then quarreled over the spoils, especially Macedonia, and fought a Second Balkan War in 1913. The Balkan wars raised the power of Serbia and heightened tensions with Austria-Hungary, which helped to set the stage for the First World War.'),
  ('Republic of China founded', 1912, 'On 1 January 1912 Sun Yat-sen was sworn in as provisional president of the Republic of China at Nanjing, after the Xinhai Revolution had overthrown the Qing dynasty. The republic rested on his ''Three Principles of the People'': nationalism, democracy and people''s livelihood. Sun soon gave up the presidency to the strongman Yuan Shikai, and China broke up into regions ruled by warlords. The republic governed the mainland until 1949, and its government has ruled Taiwan since then.'),
  ('Battle of Galicia', 1914, 'From late August to mid-September 1914 the Russian armies of the Southwestern Front fought the Austro-Hungarian armies in the Battle of Galicia, a huge series of battles over what is now southern Poland and western Ukraine. The Austro-Hungarian army, whose offensive was poorly planned, was beaten in several encounters and driven back with heavy losses, including many prisoners, and the two sides together lost about half a million men. The Russians captured Lemberg (Lviv) and besieged the fortress of Przemyśl. The old regular Austro-Hungarian army was largely destroyed in these battles, and Austria-Hungary became increasingly dependent on Germany for the rest of the war.'),
  ('Battle of Kilimanjaro', 1914, 'On 3 November 1914, as part of a British plan to invade German East Africa from two directions, about 1,500 Indian troops attacked the German position at Longido, on the slopes of Mount Kilimanjaro. The attackers climbed the hill at night and were caught in the crossfire of a smaller German force of African Schutztruppe soldiers and mounted settlers at daybreak. A German patrol then stampeded the British water mules, and the British withdrew in the dark, having achieved nothing. The same week the larger British landing at Tanga also failed, and German forces held out in East Africa until the end of the war.'),
  ('Battle of Tanga', 1914, 'From 3 to 5 November 1914 a British Indian force of about 8,000 men landed at Tanga, in German East Africa, expecting an easy victory over a few defenders. Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck''s much smaller force of German and African troops arrived by train and drove the attackers back, helped by swarms of angry bees that attacked the British soldiers, which gave the battle the name ''Battle of the Bees''. The British lost hundreds of men and abandoned weapons and ammunition on the beach, and then withdrew. The defeat encouraged Lettow-Vorbeck to continue his guerrilla war, which tied down far larger Allied forces until the end of the war.'),
  ('Battle of Veracruz', 1914, 'On 21 April 1914 U.S. Navy and Marine forces seized the Mexican port of Veracruz, after an incident at Tampico in which U.S. sailors were briefly arrested, and after news that a German ship was bringing arms to the dictator Victoriano Huerta. Some twenty Americans and well over a hundred Mexicans died, among them cadets and civilians who resisted the landing. The occupation lasted until November and helped to weaken Huerta, who resigned in July. But it also angered most Mexicans, including Huerta''s enemies, and fed anti-American feeling for decades.'),
  ('Battle of the Frontiers', 1914, 'The Battle of the Frontiers, fought from 14 to 25 August 1914, was a series of clashes in Lorraine, the Ardennes, Charleroi and Mons that opened the fighting on the Western Front. The French army, following its prewar plan of attack and relying on spirit and the bayonet, advanced in bright uniforms into German machine guns and artillery, and suffered terrible losses, including some 27,000 dead on 22 August, the bloodiest day in French military history. The Germans, advancing through Belgium under the Schlieffen Plan, forced the French and the small British army back toward Paris. The retreat ended only at the Marne, where the German advance was stopped.'),
  ('Battle of the Marne', 1914, 'From 6 to 12 September 1914 the French armies under General Joffre, joined by the British Expeditionary Force, counterattacked the advancing Germans along the Marne River, east of Paris, and stopped them. A gap opened between two German armies, and the Germans retreated dozens of kilometres to the Aisne River. The battle ended German hopes of a quick victory over France under the Schlieffen Plan, and it ended the war of movement, as both sides dug trenches. It cost about half a million casualties, and it is remembered also for the Paris taxis that brought reserves to the front.'),
  ('Battle of Łódź', 1914, 'From 11 November to 6 December 1914 a German army group tried to encircle the Russian armies around Łódź, in Poland, in a battle fought in freezing, muddy conditions. The Germans nearly trapped the Russian Second Army, but the Russians counterattacked, and German units that had advanced deeply were themselves nearly cut off and had to fight their way out. In the end the Russians abandoned Łódź but kept their army intact and held the line. The battle ended without a decisive result and cost both sides very heavy losses, and it ended Germany''s hopes of knocking Russia out of the war in 1914.'),
  ('First Battle of Ypres', 1914, 'From 19 October to 22 November 1914 German armies attacked the British, French and Belgian troops holding the Belgian town of Ypres, in the last big battle of the ''Race to the Sea''. The small British professional army, the ''Old Contemptibles'', held on at the cost of most of its trained men, and thousands of young German volunteer soldiers were killed in attacks at Langemarck, in what Germans called the ''Massacre of the Innocents''. The Allies kept Ypres, and both sides then dug in along a line from the Channel to Switzerland. The town stayed in Allied hands for the rest of the war, and its salient was the scene of further battles in 1915, 1917 and 1918.'),
  ('World War I begins', 1914, 'World War I began on 28 July 1914, when Austria-Hungary declared war on Serbia a month after the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo. Alliances drew in Russia, Germany, France and, after Germany invaded Belgium, Britain, and the war divided Europe into the Allies and the Central Powers. Over four years it killed some nine million soldiers and many millions of civilians, the Russian, German, Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman empires collapsed, and the peace settlement helped to cause the Second World War. It also brought new weapons such as tanks, aircraft and poison gas to the battlefield.'),
  ('Battle of Gallipoli', 1915, 'The Gallipoli campaign, from February 1915 to January 1916, was the Allied attempt to force the Dardanelles Strait and capture Constantinople, in order to knock the Ottoman Empire out of the war and open a sea route to Russia. A naval attack failed in March, and troops landed on 25 April, but the Ottoman defenders, among them Mustafa Kemal, held the heights, and the fighting turned into a trench stalemate. The Allies evacuated after about half a million casualties on the two sides. It was a disaster for Winston Churchill, who had backed it, but it is remembered as a founding story by Australia and New Zealand, on Anzac Day, and by Turkey, where it helped make Atatürk.'),
  ('Arab Revolt', 1916, 'The Arab Revolt began in June 1916, when Sharif Hussein of Mecca, whose family claimed descent from the Prophet, rose against Ottoman rule with British backing, hoping for an independent Arab state. His sons Faisal and Abdullah led the Arab forces, who raided the Hejaz Railway and, with the British officer T. E. Lawrence as adviser, took Aqaba in 1917. Arab troops entered Damascus in October 1918. But Britain and France had secretly agreed in 1916 to divide the region between them, and the postwar mandates left the Arabs without the single state they had expected.'),
  ('Battle of Verdun', 1916, 'The Battle of Verdun lasted from 21 February to 18 December 1916 and was the longest battle of the First World War. The German commander Falkenhayn is said to have wanted to ''bleed France white'' by attacking the fortress city of Verdun, which the French would feel they had to defend at any price. French troops, supplied along a single road later called the Sacred Way, held out under General Pétain, and ''They shall not pass'' became the rallying cry. The French recaptured most of the lost ground in the end, but around 700,000 men were killed or wounded, and Verdun became a symbol of French sacrifice.'),
  ('Battle of the Somme', 1916, 'The Battle of the Somme, from 1 July to 18 November 1916, was a British and French offensive in northern France meant to break the German line and relieve the pressure on Verdun. On the first day the British army lost about 57,000 men, including some 19,000 killed, the worst day in its history, many of them volunteers of the ''Pals'' battalions. Tanks were used for the first time in September. The Allies gained only a few kilometres at a cost of more than a million casualties on all sides, and the battle became a symbol of the slaughter of the Western Front.'),
  ('Brusilov Offensive', 1916, 'In June 1916 the Russian general Aleksei Brusilov attacked the Austro-Hungarian armies in Galicia and Bukovina on a wide front, using surprise, careful preparation and many simultaneous attacks instead of a single big push. The Russians broke through and advanced up to 100 km, and the Austro-Hungarian army lost about a million men, including many prisoners. Germany had to move troops from the Western Front, which eased the pressure on Verdun, and Romania entered the war on the Allied side. The offensive also cost Russia very heavily, and the exhaustion it caused helped to bring on the revolutions of 1917.'),
  ('Balfour Declaration', 1917, 'On 2 November 1917 the British Foreign Secretary, Arthur Balfour, wrote a letter to Lord Rothschild, a leader of the British Jewish community, stating that the government supported ''a national home for the Jewish people'' in Palestine. The letter added that nothing should be done to harm the rights of the existing non-Jewish communities, who were then the overwhelming majority. Britain wanted to win Jewish support in the war, and was conquering Palestine from the Ottomans at the time. The declaration, which later became part of the League of Nations mandate for Palestine, is a root of the Arab-Israeli conflict.'),
  ('Battle of Cambrai', 1917, 'On 20 November 1917 the British attacked the German Hindenburg Line at Cambrai with nearly 500 tanks, in the first big use of massed tanks, without the usual long artillery bombardment that would have warned the enemy. The surprise was complete, and the British advanced several kilometres in a day, so that church bells were rung in Britain. But there were too few reserves to hold the gains, and on 30 November a German counterattack using new infiltration tactics took back most of the ground. The battle showed the promise of tanks and combined-arms warfare, and the German counterattack foreshadowed their 1918 offensives.'),
  ('Battle of Caporetto', 1917, 'On 24 October 1917 German and Austro-Hungarian troops attacked the Italian army at Caporetto, now Kobarid in Slovenia, using poison gas and new infiltration tactics, and broke through the line in a few hours. The Italians retreated about 100 km to the Piave River, with some 265,000 men taken prisoner and enormous amounts of equipment lost. The disaster led to General Cadorna''s dismissal and Armando Diaz''s appointment, and the shaken army rallied to hold the Piave line. ''Caporetto'' became an Italian word for a total disaster, and the battle is the background of Ernest Hemingway''s novel A Farewell to Arms.'),
  ('Battle of Gaza', 1917, 'In 1917 the British Egyptian Expeditionary Force fought three battles to take Gaza, the gateway to Palestine from Egypt, held by the Ottoman army with German help. The first two attempts, in March and April, failed with heavy losses. In the third battle, from 31 October to 7 November, General Allenby feinted at Gaza and instead captured Beersheba in a surprise attack, including the famous charge of Australian light horsemen, and broke the Ottoman line. Gaza fell, and the British took Jerusalem in December 1917.'),
  ('Battle of Mahenge', 1917, 'The Mahenge offensive of 1917 was a campaign by the Belgian Force Publique, recruited in the Congo, which advanced on the plateau of Mahenge in German East Africa, now Tanzania, and captured it on 9 October 1917 from the German Schutztruppe. The German commander, Captain Theodor Tafel, withdrew to the south-east. The Belgians, who by then held about a third of the German colony, were stopped by rains and supply problems, and two-thirds of the 20,000 porters recruited in Ruanda-Urundi never returned home. After the war Belgium was given control of Ruanda-Urundi as a League of Nations mandate.'),
  ('Battle of Mahiwa', 1917, 'From 15 to 19 October 1917 South African and Nigerian troops under General van Deventer attacked the German column of Lettow-Vorbeck at Nyangao and Mahiwa, in German East Africa. The Germans, dug in on a ridge, drove the attackers back with heavy losses, and the British lost over 2,700 men in what has been called the most savage battle of the African campaign. But the Germans lost about a third of their force and almost all their smokeless ammunition. Lettow-Vorbeck therefore left German East Africa and led his army into Portuguese Mozambique, to continue his guerrilla war on captured supplies.'),
  ('Battle of Passchendaele', 1917, 'The Third Battle of Ypres, known as Passchendaele, was fought from 31 July to 10 November 1917, when Haig''s British and Commonwealth armies attacked in Flanders in the hope of breaking out of the Ypres salient and capturing the Belgian coast. Unusually heavy rain, and the destruction of the drainage system by shellfire, turned the battlefield into a sea of mud in which men and horses drowned. Canadian troops finally took the ruins of Passchendaele village on 6 November, about eight kilometres from where the attack started. The campaign cost about half a million casualties on both sides, a figure historians dispute, and became a symbol of the futility and suffering of the war.'),
  ('Bolshevik Revolution', 1917, 'On 7 November 1917 (25 October by the old Russian calendar) the Bolsheviks led by Vladimir Lenin seized power in Petrograd, arresting the ministers of the Provisional Government in the Winter Palace, with Red Guards and soldiers organized by Leon Trotsky. They promised ''peace, land and bread'', and soon took Russia out of the war and gave the estates to the peasants. Their rule was challenged by the Russian Civil War, which they won by 1922, creating the Soviet Union. It was the first successful communist revolution and shaped world politics for most of the twentieth century.'),
  ('Russian Revolution', 1917, 'In February 1917 (March by the modern calendar) strikes and mutinies in Petrograd forced Tsar Nicholas II to abdicate, ending three centuries of Romanov rule. A Provisional Government kept Russia in the First World War, which made it unpopular, and shared power with the workers'' and soldiers'' councils, the soviets. In October the Bolsheviks under Lenin took power, and a bitter civil war followed, which they won. The Russian Revolution created the Soviet Union and the first communist state, and it inspired and alarmed the world for the following decades.'),
  ('Battle of Aleppo', 1918, 'On 25 October 1918 Arab forces under Prince Faisal captured Aleppo in the pursuit of the retreating Ottoman army north from Damascus, in which British cavalry and armoured cars also took part. The next day British and Indian cavalry charged the Ottoman rearguard at Haritan, north-west of the city, the last major action of the campaign. Within days the Ottoman Empire signed the Armistice of Mudros on 30 October, ending its war. The capture of Aleppo marked the end of the fighting in the Middle Eastern theatre of the First World War.'),
  ('Battle of Damascus', 1918, 'On 1 October 1918, after Allenby''s victory at Megiddo, Australian light horsemen and Arab forces under Prince Faisal entered Damascus as the Ottoman troops and their German allies retreated. The Arab forces, with the British officer T. E. Lawrence, had harassed the Ottoman rear and wanted the city to be seen as taken by the Arabs. The fall of Damascus ended some four hundred years of Ottoman rule over Syria. But the Arabs'' hope of an independent kingdom there was soon defeated, as France took control of Syria in 1920.'),
  ('Battle of Namacurra', 1918, 'From 1 to 3 July 1918 a German force under Lettow-Vorbeck attacked the Portuguese and British garrison of Namacurra, in central Mozambique, which guarded a large new supply depot. Three Portuguese battalions with two British companies resisted harder than other Portuguese units had done, but they were pushed back and forced to retreat across the Namacurra River, where about a hundred men drowned. It was the deadliest engagement of Lettow-Vorbeck''s long campaign, and the Germans captured large stocks of arms, ammunition and food, and some 540 prisoners. The defeat greatly weakened Portugal''s war effort in Africa, and Lettow-Vorbeck went on to surrender only after the Armistice.'),
  ('Hundred Days Offensive', 1918, 'The Hundred Days Offensive, from 8 August to 11 November 1918, was the series of Allied attacks that ended the First World War on the Western Front. It began at Amiens, where British, Australian, Canadian and French troops with hundreds of tanks and aircraft broke the German line, a day that the German commander Ludendorff called the ''black day of the German Army''. American, British and French armies then pushed the Germans back along the whole front and broke the Hindenburg Line. Germany, with its armies in retreat and its allies collapsing, asked for an armistice, which was signed on 11 November.'),
  ('Spring Offensive', 1918, 'On 21 March 1918 the Germans under Ludendorff launched the Spring Offensive, their last great attempt to win the First World War before American troops arrived in force. Using short, intense bombardments and elite stormtroopers who infiltrated the Allied trenches, they broke through the British lines and advanced up to 60 km, the biggest gains on the Western Front since 1914. But the advance outran its supplies and reserves, and the Germans lost nearly 700,000 men, many of them their best soldiers, without winning a decisive victory. The Allies, who had now put their armies under the single command of Marshal Foch, then counterattacked in the summer and won.'),
  ('May Fourth Movement', 1919, 'On 4 May 1919 some 3,000 students marched to Tiananmen in Beijing to protest the Treaty of Versailles, which gave Germany''s rights in Shandong province to Japan instead of returning them to China. The protests spread to other cities, with strikes and boycotts of Japanese goods, and the Chinese government refused to sign the treaty. The movement was also part of a wider ''New Culture'' drive for science, democracy, modern writing in plain language and an end to old Confucian traditions. It shaped a generation of nationalists and radicals, among them the founders of the Chinese Communist Party.'),
  ('Treaty of Versailles', 1919, 'The Treaty of Versailles, signed on 28 June 1919, exactly five years after the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, ended the First World War between Germany and the Allies. Germany lost territory, including Alsace-Lorraine, had to give up its colonies, limit its army to 100,000 men, and accept responsibility for the war, which was the basis for large reparations payments. The treaty also created the League of Nations. Many Germans saw it as a humiliating ''Diktat'', and the resentment helped extremists such as Hitler to rise in the following years.'),
  ('Battle of Warsaw', 1920, 'In August 1920, during the Polish-Soviet War, the Red Army under Tukhachevsky advanced to the gates of Warsaw, hoping to carry communist revolution westward to Germany and the rest of Europe. Józef Piłsudski''s Poles, who had broken Soviet radio codes, counterattacked from the south with a surprise strike into the rear of the Soviet armies. The Red Army was routed in what the Poles call the ''Miracle on the Vistula'', and thousands of its soldiers were captured or fled into East Prussia. The victory saved Polish independence and stopped the westward advance of Bolshevism.'),
  ('League of Nations founded', 1920, 'The League of Nations, founded on 10 January 1920 by the Paris Peace Conference that ended the First World War, was the first worldwide organization created to keep the peace by collective security and arbitration. It was the idea of U.S. President Woodrow Wilson, but the U.S. Senate refused to join, which weakened it from the start. The League settled some small disputes, but it could not stop aggression by Japan in Manchuria, by Italy in Ethiopia or by Nazi Germany. It was dissolved in 1946, and its place was taken by the United Nations.'),
  ('Treaty of Trianon', 1920, 'The Treaty of Trianon, signed on 4 June 1920 at the Grand Trianon palace in Versailles, was the peace treaty between the Allies and Hungary after the First World War. Hungary lost about two thirds of its pre-war territory, including Transylvania and Slovakia, and some three million ethnic Hungarians found themselves in neighbouring countries. It was also left with a small army and no access to the sea. Hungarians remember it as a national tragedy, and the wish to undo it fed Hungarian nationalism and later pushed Hungary into alliance with Germany.'),
  ('Women gain federal suffrage in U.S.', 1920, 'The 19th Amendment to the U.S. Constitution, ratified on 18 August 1920 when Tennessee became the 36th state to approve it, guaranteed that the right to vote could not be denied on account of sex. It was the result of a campaign that began at the Seneca Falls Convention in 1848 and included leaders such as Susan B. Anthony, Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Alice Paul, with marches, hunger strikes and imprisonments. Millions of American women could vote in that year''s presidential election. Many Black women in the South, however, were kept from the polls by discriminatory laws until the Voting Rights Act of 1965.'),
  ('Chinese Communist Party founded', 1921, 'The Chinese Communist Party was founded at a secret congress held in Shanghai in July 1921, which finished on a tourist boat on a lake at Jiaxing after the police raided the meeting. About a dozen delegates took part, among them the young Mao Zedong, representing only some fifty members, with advice from agents of the Soviet-led Comintern. The Party''s early leaders were the scholars Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao. It later led the Long March, won the civil war in 1949 under Mao and has ruled mainland China since then.'),
  ('End of Ottoman Empire', 1922, 'On 1 November 1922 the Grand National Assembly in Ankara, led by Mustafa Kemal, abolished the Ottoman sultanate, ending an empire that had lasted since about 1299. The last sultan, Mehmed VI, left Constantinople on a British warship on 17 November. The Republic of Turkey was proclaimed in October 1923, and the caliphate was abolished in 1924. The empire''s former Arab provinces had by then become mandates under British and French control.'),
  ('Rise of fascism in Italy', 1922, 'In October 1922 Benito Mussolini''s Fascist Blackshirts threatened to march on Rome, and King Victor Emmanuel III, who refused to use the army against them, named Mussolini prime minister. Within a few years Mussolini destroyed Italy''s democracy, banned rival parties and ruled as the dictator ''Il Duce''. His regime, which glorified the nation and violence, became a model for other movements such as Hitler''s Nazis. Mussolini later invaded Ethiopia, allied himself with Germany and brought Italy into the Second World War, and he was overthrown in 1943.'),
  ('Treaty of Lausanne', 1923, 'The Treaty of Lausanne, signed on 24 July 1923, was the peace treaty that replaced the Treaty of Sèvres of 1920, which the Turkish nationalists had rejected after winning their War of Independence. It recognized the new Turkey''s borders and full sovereignty, ended the foreign privileges known as the capitulations, and made no provision for the Armenian or Kurdish homelands promised earlier. A compulsory population exchange sent about 1.2 million Orthodox Christians from Turkey to Greece, and some 400,000 Muslims from Greece to Turkey. It is seen as the greatest diplomatic success of Mustafa Kemal Atatürk.'),
  ('Great Depression begins', 1929, 'The Great Depression is usually dated from the Wall Street crash of October 1929, when share prices collapsed after a speculative boom. Thousands of banks failed, businesses closed and by 1933 about a quarter of American workers were unemployed. The slump spread around the world as trade and credit collapsed, and mass unemployment in Germany helped the Nazis to power. It led to Franklin Roosevelt''s New Deal in the United States and a new belief that governments must manage the economy, and it ended only with the Second World War.'),
  ('Creation of Saudi Arabia', 1932, 'On 23 September 1932 King Abdulaziz ibn Saud proclaimed the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, joining the regions of Hejaz and Najd that he had conquered over thirty years, starting with Riyadh in 1902. His warriors had taken Mecca and Medina in 1924-1925 from the Hashemite family, and he became guardian of Islam''s two holiest cities. The kingdom was then poor, but the discovery of oil in 1938 made it one of the richest and most influential states in the Middle East. It is still ruled by his descendants.'),
  ('Nazi seizure of power', 1933, 'On 30 January 1933 President Hindenburg appointed Adolf Hitler, the leader of the Nazi Party, as Chancellor of Germany, at the head of a coalition government. After the Reichstag fire in February, Hitler used emergency decrees to jail opponents, and the Enabling Act of 23 March 1933 gave his government the power to make laws without parliament. Within months other parties and the trade unions were banned, and when Hindenburg died in 1934 Hitler made himself Führer. His dictatorship led to the Second World War and the Holocaust.'),
  ('New Deal', 1933, 'The New Deal was the group of programs and reforms that President Franklin D. Roosevelt introduced from 1933 to fight the Great Depression, when about a quarter of American workers were unemployed. It brought relief through public works such as the Civilian Conservation Corps and the Works Progress Administration, recovery measures such as the Tennessee Valley Authority, and reforms such as bank deposit insurance, stock market regulation and Social Security in 1935. Unemployment fell, though the Depression only ended with the war. The New Deal permanently expanded the role of the federal government in American life.'),
  ('Long March', 1934, 'The Long March was the retreat of the Chinese Communist Red Army from October 1934 to October 1935, after Chiang Kai-shek''s Nationalist armies had surrounded its base in Jiangxi. About 100,000 people set out and marched some 9,000 km through mountains, rivers and swamps, harassed by the enemy, and perhaps only one in ten reached the new base in Shaanxi. During the march, at the Zunyi Conference in January 1935, Mao Zedong became the leading figure in the Party. The march became a founding legend of Chinese Communism, and the survivors formed the core of the leadership that won power in 1949.'),
  ('Spanish Civil War', 1936, 'The Spanish Civil War began in July 1936, when a group of generals, among them Francisco Franco, rebelled against the elected Popular Front government of the Second Republic. The Nationalists were helped by Nazi Germany and Fascist Italy, whose aircraft bombed towns such as Guernica, while the Republic was aided by the Soviet Union and by volunteers of the International Brigades, as Britain and France stayed neutral. Franco won in April 1939 after a war that killed hundreds of thousands of people, and he ruled Spain as dictator until his death in 1975. The war was seen as a rehearsal for the Second World War.'),
  ('Battle of Nanjing', 1937, 'After taking Shanghai, Japanese armies advanced on Nanjing, the Chinese capital, and attacked it in early December 1937. The Chinese defenders, tens of thousands of men under General Tang Shengzhi, were poorly organized, and the defence collapsed within days, with many troops trapped by the Yangtze River as they tried to flee. Japanese troops entered the city on 13 December, and the Chinese government had already moved its seat inland, first to Wuhan and then to Chongqing. The fall of Nanjing was followed by the Nanjing Massacre.'),
  ('Battle of Shanghai', 1937, 'From 13 August to 26 November 1937 Chinese and Japanese forces fought the Battle of Shanghai, the first major battle of the Second Sino-Japanese War and one of its largest. Chiang Kai-shek sent his best German-trained divisions to hold the city, partly to draw foreign attention to China''s plight. The fighting was fierce, with street fights, naval gunfire and air attacks, and Japan, which had boasted of conquering China in three months, needed three months just to take Shanghai. The Chinese lost most of their best troops, but the battle showed that China would not collapse quickly.'),
  ('Nanjing Massacre', 1937, 'After capturing Nanjing on 13 December 1937, Japanese troops killed large numbers of Chinese prisoners of war and civilians and raped tens of thousands of women over about six weeks. Estimates of the dead range from about 40,000 to more than 300,000, the figure given by China. A small group of foreigners, among them the German businessman John Rabe, set up a Safety Zone that sheltered some 250,000 civilians. The massacre remains a deeply sensitive issue between China and Japan.'),
  ('Second Sino-Japanese War', 1937, 'The Second Sino-Japanese War began on 7 July 1937, when a clash at the Marco Polo Bridge near Beijing led to full-scale fighting, following Japan''s seizure of Manchuria in 1931. Japan took Beijing, Shanghai, Nanjing and Wuhan, but the Chinese, with Nationalists and Communists in an uneasy united front, would not surrender and tied down over a million Japanese troops. The war became part of the Second World War after Pearl Harbor in 1941 and ended with Japan''s surrender in 1945. Some 15 to 20 million Chinese died, and the war weakened the Nationalists and strengthened the Communists, who won the civil war in 1949.'),
  ('Battle of Wuhan', 1938, 'From June to October 1938 Japan fought the Battle of Wuhan, the largest, longest and bloodiest battle of the Second Sino-Japanese War, to take the cities of Wuhan on the Yangtze River. China defended with about a million men, using the river, the mountains and the flooding of the Yellow River in June to slow the Japanese advance, which killed many civilians. Japan took the cities in late October, but at heavy cost, and did not destroy the Chinese army, which withdrew inland to Chongqing. After Wuhan the war settled into a long stalemate, as Japan''s forces were stretched too far.'),
  ('Munich Agreement', 1938, 'On 30 September 1938 Hitler, Mussolini, the British prime minister Neville Chamberlain and the French premier Daladier agreed at Munich that Germany would annex the Sudetenland, the border region of Czechoslovakia with a mainly German-speaking population. Czechoslovakia, which was not invited to the talks, was forced to accept, and it lost its mountain fortifications. Chamberlain came home declaring that he had brought ''peace for our time''. But in March 1939 Hitler occupied the rest of the country, and the war came in September, so ''Munich'' became a byword for the failure of appeasement.'),
  ('Battle of Khalkhin Gol', 1939, 'From May to September 1939 Soviet-Mongolian and Japanese forces fought along the Khalkhin Gol river, on the border between Mongolia and Japanese-held Manchukuo, in a conflict the Japanese call the Nomonhan Incident. In late August the Soviet commander Georgy Zhukov, with tanks, aircraft and artillery, encircled and destroyed the Japanese 23rd Division. The ceasefire of September 1939 left the Soviets victorious, and the defeat helped to convince Japan to look south toward Southeast Asia, not north against the USSR. This freed Stalin to move troops west when Germany attacked in 1941, and it made Zhukov''s reputation.'),
  ('World War II begins in Europe', 1939, 'World War II began in Europe on 1 September 1939, when Germany invaded Poland, days after the Nazi-Soviet Pact had secretly divided Eastern Europe between Hitler and Stalin. Britain and France declared war on Germany two days later, and the Soviet Union invaded Poland from the east on 17 September. The war spread across Europe, Africa, Asia and the oceans, and lasted until 1945. It was the deadliest conflict in history, killing some 70 to 85 million people, among them six million Jews murdered in the Holocaust.'),
  ('Battle of Britain', 1940, 'From July to October 1940 the German Luftwaffe tried to win control of the skies over Britain, as the first step of a planned invasion. The Royal Air Force, with its Spitfire and Hurricane fighters and a pioneering radar warning and control system, defended against a larger force, and when the Germans switched in September to bombing London the RAF''s airfields got relief. Hitler postponed the invasion, so it was his first major defeat. Churchill said of the pilots that never had so many owed so much to so few.'),
  ('Fall of France', 1940, 'From 10 May to 25 June 1940 Germany conquered France and the Low Countries in just six weeks. The German tank forces went through the Ardennes forest, which the Allies thought impassable, and reached the English Channel in ten days, bypassing the Maginot Line and cutting off the best Allied armies in Belgium. More than 330,000 British and French soldiers escaped from Dunkirk, but Paris fell on 14 June, and France signed an armistice on 22 June. The country was split into an occupied zone and a collaborating regime at Vichy, while General de Gaulle continued the fight from London.'),
  ('Attack on Pearl Harbor', 1941, 'On the morning of 7 December 1941 Japanese carrier aircraft launched a surprise attack on the U.S. Pacific Fleet at Pearl Harbor, in Hawaii, while negotiations were still going on. More than 2,400 Americans were killed, and four battleships were sunk and four more damaged, along with many aircraft destroyed. Roosevelt called it ''a date which will live in infamy'', and the next day Congress declared war on Japan. Germany and Italy then declared war on the United States, so that the attack turned the war into a truly global conflict.'),
  ('Battle of Hong Kong', 1941, 'Hours after Pearl Harbor, on 8 December 1941, Japanese forces attacked the British colony of Hong Kong from the Chinese mainland. The defenders, about 14,000 British, Indian, Canadian and local volunteer troops, held out for 17 days, but without air or naval support, and with their water supply cut, they were forced to surrender on Christmas Day. It was the first battle of the war for Canadian troops. The Japanese occupation lasted three years and eight months, with great hardship and atrocities for the population.'),
  ('Battle of Kiev', 1941, 'In August and September 1941 German armies under Guderian and Kleist closed a giant pincer around the Soviet Southwestern Front east of Kiev, in Ukraine, trapping four Soviet armies. Stalin had refused his generals'' requests to withdraw, and when the ring closed around Lokhvytsia on 16 September, hundreds of thousands of Soviet soldiers were killed or captured, some 600,000 in all. It was the largest encirclement in history, and it gave Germany Kiev and Ukraine''s resources. But it delayed the attack on Moscow by weeks, which helped to save the Soviet capital before the winter.'),
  ('Battle of Malaya', 1941, 'The Malayan campaign began on 8 December 1941, when Japanese troops under General Yamashita landed in southern Thailand and northern Malaya, at the start of the Pacific War. Moving through the jungle on bicycles and with tanks and air superiority, the Japanese pushed the larger British, Indian and Australian forces back down the peninsula, about 1,100 km, in less than two months. The Royal Navy lost the battleship Prince of Wales and the battle cruiser Repulse to air attack on 10 December. The campaign ended with the fall of Singapore, and about 130,000 Allied soldiers were killed, wounded or captured.'),
  ('Battle of Smolensk', 1941, 'From July to September 1941 the German Army Group Centre fought around Smolensk, on the road to Moscow, in the second phase of Operation Barbarossa. The Germans encircled large Soviet forces and took some 300,000 prisoners, but they could not completely destroy the Soviet armies, which fought fiercely and counterattacked repeatedly. The Germans lost time, men and tanks, and Hitler turned some of his forces south to Kiev instead of going straight for Moscow. The battle delayed the German advance on Moscow by weeks, and many historians see it as an early sign that the plan for a quick victory would fail.'),
  ('Battle of South Shanxi', 1941, 'In May 1941 about 200,000 Japanese troops attacked the Chinese Nationalist armies holding the Zhongtiao Mountains in southern Shanxi, the largest area north of the Yellow River still in Chinese hands. Using infiltration along mountain paths and a move along the river bank behind the Chinese, the Japanese encircled the defenders, who had become complacent, and many units broke up. The Communist Eighth Route Army did not help, as relations with the Nationalists were at a low after the New Fourth Army incident of January 1941. China lost tens of thousands of men and the mountains, and the defeat is remembered as one of the worst of the whole war.'),
  ('Operation Barbarossa', 1941, 'On 22 June 1941 Nazi Germany and its allies invaded the Soviet Union with some 3.8 million men on a front of nearly 3,000 km, breaking the non-aggression pact of 1939. The surprise was almost total, and in the first months the Germans destroyed whole Soviet armies and took millions of prisoners, reaching the suburbs of Moscow by December. But the Soviets did not collapse, and a counterattack on 5 December drove the Germans back from Moscow. The war in the East became the largest and bloodiest theatre of the Second World War, and it included the mass murder of Jews and Soviet prisoners.'),
  ('Pearl Harbor attack', 1941, 'The attack on Pearl Harbor was the work of Admiral Yamamoto, who hoped to cripple the U.S. Pacific Fleet long enough for Japan to seize the oil and resources of Southeast Asia, after the United States had cut off its oil supplies in 1941. Six Japanese carriers sent more than 350 aircraft in two waves against the ships and airfields on Oahu. But the American aircraft carriers were at sea, and the fuel tanks and repair yards were untouched, so most of the damaged battleships were later repaired. Instead of breaking American will, the attack united the country behind the war, and Japan''s gamble ended in defeat in 1945.'),
  ('Battle of Coral Sea', 1942, 'The Battle of the Coral Sea, from 4 to 8 May 1942, was the first naval battle in which the opposing ships never saw or fired at each other, as the fighting was done entirely by carrier aircraft. American and Australian forces tried to stop a Japanese invasion fleet heading for Port Moresby, in New Guinea. The Americans lost the carrier Lexington, while the Japanese lost a light carrier, and the damage to two other Japanese carriers kept them out of the next battle, at Midway. It was a tactical draw, but a strategic victory for the Allies, because the invasion of Port Moresby was called off.'),
  ('Battle of Guadalcanal', 1942, 'The Guadalcanal campaign began on 7 August 1942, when U.S. Marines landed on the island in the Solomons to seize an airfield that the Japanese were building. For six months the two sides fought a bitter battle on land, sea and air, with Japanese attempts to retake the island defeated at Bloody Ridge and in a series of naval battles. Japanese soldiers died in large numbers of disease and hunger as well as in combat, and Japan evacuated its remaining troops in February 1943. It was the first big Allied offensive against Japan and a turning point of the Pacific War.'),
  ('Battle of Kharkov', 1942, 'In May 1942 the Soviet Marshal Timoshenko launched an offensive to retake Kharkov, in Ukraine, but the Germans struck the flanks of the Soviet advance and trapped it in a pocket south of the city, near Izium. Some 280,000 Soviet soldiers were lost, more than 170,000 of them killed or captured, along with huge amounts of equipment. It was one of the worst Soviet defeats of the war. It cleared the way for the German summer offensive of 1942 toward Stalingrad and the Caucasus.'),
  ('Battle of Midway', 1942, 'On 4 June 1942, six months after Pearl Harbor, American carrier aircraft sank four Japanese aircraft carriers, Akagi, Kaga, Soryu and Hiryu, near Midway Island in the Pacific. U.S. codebreakers had discovered the Japanese plan, so Admiral Nimitz was able to ambush the fleet, and in a few minutes dive bombers fatally damaged three carriers whose decks were full of fuelled and armed planes. Japan also lost hundreds of aircraft and many of its best pilots and mechanics, while the Americans lost the carrier Yorktown. Midway ended Japan''s string of victories and is seen as the turning point of the Pacific War.'),
  ('Battle of Singapore', 1942, 'From 8 to 15 February 1942 Japanese troops under General Yamashita crossed the Johor Strait and took Singapore, the main British naval base in Asia, which had been called an impregnable fortress. Its big guns pointed out to sea, and the Japanese attacked from the land side, through Malaya. After a week of fighting, and with water and ammunition running out, General Percival surrendered some 80,000 British, Indian and Australian troops. It was the largest surrender in British military history, and it destroyed the image of European colonial power in Asia.'),
  ('Battle of Stalingrad', 1942, 'From August 1942 to February 1943 German and Soviet armies fought for Stalingrad, the industrial city on the Volga River that bore Stalin''s name. The Germans took most of the city in brutal house-to-house fighting, but on 19 November the Red Army launched a huge counteroffensive that encircled the German Sixth Army and its allies, about 300,000 men. Hitler refused to let them break out, and the last survivors, some 90,000 men, surrendered in early February 1943. Stalingrad was Germany''s greatest defeat to that point, and the turning point of the war in Europe.'),
  ('Naval Battle of Guadalcanal', 1942, 'From 12 to 15 November 1942 American and Japanese warships fought a series of fierce night and day actions near Guadalcanal that decided the struggle for the island. The U.S. Navy stopped the Japanese battleships from bombarding the airfield, Henderson Field, and wrecked most of a convoy of troop transports, so Japan failed to land its reinforcements. Two U.S. admirals, Callaghan and Scott, were killed in the savage night fighting, and the five Sullivan brothers died when the cruiser Juneau was sunk. Japan lost two battleships and many other ships, and after this it could no longer hope to win Guadalcanal.'),
  ('Battle of Kursk', 1943, 'The Battle of Kursk, fought from July to August 1943, was Germany''s last big offensive on the Eastern Front. The Germans attacked a huge bulge in the Soviet line around Kursk, hoping to destroy the Soviet forces there, but the Soviets, who knew of the plan, had prepared deep belts of minefields, trenches and anti-tank guns. The German attack stalled within days, in huge tank clashes around Prokhorovka, and the Soviets then launched counteroffensives. After Kursk the Red Army held the initiative, and the Germans never again launched a major strategic offensive in the East.'),
  ('Battle of Guam', 1944, 'From 21 July to 10 August 1944 American Marines and soldiers retook Guam, an American island in the Marianas that Japan had captured in December 1941, in the same campaign as Saipan. The Japanese garrison of nearly 19,000 men fought almost to the last man. The Chamorro people, who had suffered harsh treatment under three years of occupation, celebrate 21 July as Liberation Day. Guam became a vast base for the B-29 bombers that attacked Japan, and the last Japanese soldier to give up there, Shoichi Yokoi, was found hiding in the jungle in 1972.'),
  ('Battle of Leyte Gulf', 1944, 'The Battle of Leyte Gulf, fought on 23-26 October 1944, was the largest naval battle of the Second World War, and by some measures of all time, with over 200,000 sailors taking part. Japan sent almost its whole fleet, in a desperate plan to destroy the American ships supporting the invasion of the Philippines, but in four separate actions the U.S. Navy sank four carriers, three battleships, including the giant Musashi, and many other ships. The Japanese also used kamikaze suicide aircraft in an organized way for the first time. After Leyte Gulf the Japanese navy was no longer an effective fighting force.'),
  ('Battle of the Philippine Sea', 1944, 'On 19-20 June 1944, while American troops fought on Saipan, the U.S. Fifth Fleet under Admiral Spruance met the Japanese Mobile Fleet in the Philippine Sea, in the greatest carrier battle in history. American fighters, guided by radar, shot down some 400 Japanese aircraft in one day with small losses, in what pilots called the ''Great Marianas Turkey Shoot''. U.S. submarines sank two Japanese carriers, and on the second day American planes sank a third, then had to land in darkness at the limit of their fuel. Japan lost most of its experienced carrier pilots and could never again fight a large carrier battle.'),
  ('D-Day', 1944, 'On 6 June 1944, D-Day, the Allies landed some 156,000 American, British and Canadian troops on five beaches in Normandy, France, in the largest seaborne invasion in history. The landings were preceded by airborne drops behind the beaches and supported by thousands of ships and aircraft, and a deception plan had led the Germans to expect the main attack at Calais. The U.S. troops at Omaha Beach suffered the heaviest losses, but by the evening all five beaches were held. The landings opened the long-awaited second front in Western Europe, and Paris was liberated in August.'),
  ('Operation Bagration', 1944, 'Operation Bagration, launched on 22 June 1944, three years to the day after the German invasion, was the Soviet offensive in Belarus that destroyed the German Army Group Centre. A deception plan had persuaded the Germans to expect the main attack in Ukraine, and the Red Army, with about 2.4 million men, broke through on a wide front and advanced some 600 km in two months. The Germans lost hundreds of thousands of men, and 28 divisions were destroyed. It was one of the worst German defeats of the war, and it brought the Red Army to the Vistula and the outskirts of Warsaw.'),
  ('Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki', 1945, 'On 6 August 1945 a U.S. B-29 bomber dropped the first atomic bomb on the Japanese city of Hiroshima, and on 9 August a second one on Nagasaki. Together they killed between 150,000 and 250,000 people by the end of the year, mostly civilians, and many more died later of radiation. The Soviet Union declared war on Japan between the two bombings, and Japan announced its surrender on 15 August. They are the only use of nuclear weapons in war, and historians still debate whether they were needed to end the war, but they began the nuclear age.'),
  ('Battle of Berlin', 1945, 'From 16 April to 2 May 1945 the Red Army, with some two and a half million men under Marshals Zhukov and Konev, attacked Berlin, which was defended by the remnants of the German army, the SS, the Volkssturm militia and Hitler Youth boys. After the Soviets broke through the Seelow Heights, the city was surrounded and fought street by street, with the Reichstag taken on 30 April. Hitler killed himself that day in his bunker, and the garrison surrendered on 2 May. Germany''s unconditional surrender followed on 8 May, and the battle cost hundreds of thousands of casualties.'),
  ('Battle of Iwo Jima', 1945, 'From 19 February to 26 March 1945 U.S. Marines fought to take Iwo Jima, a small volcanic island whose airfields were needed for the bombing of Japan by B-29s. About 21,000 Japanese defenders under General Kuribayashi, dug into tunnels and caves, fought almost to the last man, and the Americans lost about 6,800 killed in some of the fiercest fighting of the Pacific War. On 23 February a photograph of Marines raising the American flag on Mount Suribachi became the most famous picture of the war. The island''s airfield saved the lives of many bomber crews by serving as an emergency landing place.'),
  ('Battle of Manchuria', 1945, 'On 8-9 August 1945, between the two atomic bombings, the Soviet Union declared war on Japan and invaded Manchuria with about 1.5 million men in a huge three-pronged attack. The Soviets used tanks, aircraft and fast-moving forces, in the style of the German blitzkrieg, against the Japanese Kwantung Army, which was much weaker than it had been, and it collapsed within days. More than half a million Japanese soldiers were captured, many of them held in labour camps in Siberia for years. The attack added to the pressure that led to Japan''s surrender, and the weapons captured helped the Chinese Communists in their civil war.'),
  ('Battle of Manila', 1945, 'From 3 February to 3 March 1945 American and Filipino forces fought to retake Manila, the capital of the Philippines, from Japanese troops who had been ordered to hold it. About 16,000 Japanese naval troops under Admiral Iwabuchi fought to the death in the ruined city, and Japanese soldiers murdered civilians in large numbers, while American artillery also destroyed many buildings and killed many others. Roughly 100,000 Filipino civilians died, and the city was left one of the most devastated of the war. The battle ended the Japanese occupation of the city, but at a terrible cost.'),
  ('Battle of Okinawa', 1945, 'The Battle of Okinawa, from 1 April to 22 June 1945, was the largest amphibious assault of the Pacific War, the last big battle before an expected invasion of Japan itself. About 180,000 American troops fought a Japanese army of about 100,000 dug into caves and ridges in the south of the island, and Japanese kamikaze aircraft sank some 36 ships and damaged hundreds more. The giant Japanese battleship Yamato was sunk on a one-way mission on 7 April. The battle killed some 12,000 Americans, over 100,000 Japanese and tens of thousands of Okinawan civilians, and its cost made American leaders fear what an invasion of the Japanese home islands would bring.'),
  ('Battle of Saigon', 1945, 'After Japan''s surrender in August 1945, the Allies agreed that British-Indian forces would disarm the Japanese in southern Vietnam, while Chinese forces did so in the north. On 23 September the British under General Gracey helped French troops seize control of Saigon from the Vietnamese independence government, and fighting spread with the Viet Minh and other groups. By March 1946 British, Indian and French forces had crushed the resistance in the south and the French had reasserted control. It was the first act of the conflict that grew into the First Indochina War.'),
  ('Fall of Berlin', 1945, 'The fall of Berlin in May 1945 ended Nazi Germany. Adolf Hitler killed himself in his bunker on 30 April, as Soviet troops fought a few hundred metres away, and the Berlin garrison under General Weidling surrendered on 2 May. Germany''s unconditional surrender followed on 7-8 May, celebrated as Victory in Europe Day, and the Third Reich that Hitler had said would last a thousand years had lasted twelve. The ruined city, and Germany, were divided into four occupation zones, which laid the ground for the Cold War division of Berlin.'),
  ('Nuremberg Trials', 1945, 'The Nuremberg trials were held by the four victorious Allies, the United States, the Soviet Union, Britain and France, to try the leading Nazis for crimes in the Second World War. In the main trial, from November 1945 to October 1946, twelve of the 22 defendants were sentenced to death, seven received prison terms and three were acquitted, while Hermann Göring killed himself before his execution. The court defined crimes against peace, war crimes and crimes against humanity, and rejected the defence that the accused were only following orders. The trials became the foundation of modern international criminal law and of later courts such as the International Criminal Court.'),
  ('United Nations founded', 1945, 'The United Nations Charter was signed on 26 June 1945 in San Francisco by 50 countries, and the organization came into being on 24 October 1945, when the Charter took effect. It was created to replace the failed League of Nations and to prevent another world war, with a General Assembly where every member has a vote and a Security Council in which the five victorious powers hold a veto. Beyond peacekeeping, it works on human rights, development and humanitarian aid, and in 1948 adopted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. It has grown from 51 original members to 193 today.'),
  ('Vietnamese independence declaration', 1945, 'On 2 September 1945, in Ba Dinh Square in Hanoi, Ho Chi Minh read the Declaration of Independence of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam, a few days after the Japanese surrender and the August Revolution. He opened by quoting the American Declaration of Independence and the French Declaration of the Rights of Man, to claim that Vietnam had the same rights. France refused to accept it and tried to restore its colonial rule. That began the First Indochina War, and the Vietnam War that followed.'),
  ('First Indochina War', 1946, 'The First Indochina War began in December 1946, when fighting broke out in Hanoi between French forces and the Viet Minh of Ho Chi Minh, and lasted until 1954. The Viet Minh, led militarily by General Giap, fought a guerrilla war from the countryside that grew into a conventional one, with aid from Communist China after 1949, while the United States came to pay most of the French war costs. France, weakened at home and facing a long war, was decisively beaten at Dien Bien Phu in May 1954. The Geneva Accords then divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel, which set the stage for the Vietnam War.'),
  ('Indian independence', 1947, 'At midnight on 14-15 August 1947 India became independent after nearly two centuries of British rule, which had begun with the East India Company and continued under the Crown after the revolt of 1857. Jawaharlal Nehru, the first prime minister, spoke of India''s ''tryst with destiny'', and the long non-violent campaign of Mahatma Gandhi and the Congress Party had at last succeeded. But independence came with the partition of the subcontinent into India and Pakistan, and with terrible violence. India''s independence began the end of the European colonial empires in Asia and Africa.'),
  ('Partition of India', 1947, 'In August 1947 British India was divided into two new countries, India, with a Hindu majority, and Pakistan, with a Muslim majority, in two parts, West and East Pakistan. The borders, drawn in only a few weeks by a commission under Cyril Radcliffe, cut through Punjab and Bengal and left millions on the wrong side. About 15 million people fled across the new frontiers, in one of the largest migrations in history, and perhaps a million or more were killed in violence between Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs. The partition left lasting hostility between India and Pakistan, notably over Kashmir, and in 1971 East Pakistan broke away as Bangladesh.'),
  ('Truman Doctrine', 1947, 'On 12 March 1947 President Harry Truman asked the U.S. Congress for $400 million in aid for Greece and Turkey, after Britain announced it could no longer afford to support them. Greece was fighting a communist insurgency, and Turkey was under Soviet pressure. Truman declared that the United States would support free peoples resisting attempted subjugation by armed minorities or outside pressure. The Truman Doctrine began the policy of ''containment'' of communism, and was the first step in America''s postwar commitment to Europe, followed by the Marshall Plan and NATO.'),
  ('Creation of Israel', 1948, 'On 14 May 1948, as the British Mandate for Palestine ended, David Ben-Gurion proclaimed the State of Israel in Tel Aviv. It followed the UN plan of November 1947 to partition Palestine into a Jewish and an Arab state, which the Arab side rejected. For the Jewish people it was the fulfilment of the Zionist dream and a refuge after the Holocaust, while for the Palestinians it began the Nakba, the ''catastrophe'', in which some 700,000 fled or were expelled from their homes. The creation of Israel and its aftermath have been at the centre of Middle Eastern politics since.'),
  ('First Arab-Israeli War', 1948, 'The first Arab-Israeli War began on 15 May 1948, the day after Israel declared independence, when the armies of Egypt, Jordan, Syria, Iraq and Lebanon entered the former British Mandate of Palestine. Israel, which had been fighting local Arabs since the previous November, held and then expanded its territory, and armistice agreements were signed in 1949. Jordan took the West Bank and East Jerusalem, Egypt took the Gaza Strip, and no Arab state of Palestine was created. About 6,000 Israelis, nearly one per cent of the population, were killed, and some 700,000 Palestinians became refugees.'),
  ('Marshall Plan', 1948, 'The Marshall Plan, announced by U.S. Secretary of State George Marshall in 1947 and passed by Congress in April 1948, gave about $13 billion in aid to 17 Western European countries to rebuild after the war. Its aim was to revive their economies, so that poverty would not push voters toward communism, and to create markets for American goods. The Soviet Union refused it and made its satellites do the same, which deepened the division of Europe. The plan helped Western Europe to recover quickly and began the habit of economic cooperation among its countries.'),
  ('Chinese Communist victory', 1949, 'In the civil war that resumed after 1945, Mao Zedong''s Communist People''s Liberation Army defeated Chiang Kai-shek''s Nationalist forces, which had more soldiers and American weapons but were weakened by corruption, inflation and defections. The decisive victories in Manchuria and at Huaihai in 1948-49 opened the road south. On 1 October 1949 Mao proclaimed the People''s Republic of China in Beijing, declaring that the Chinese people had stood up, while Chiang''s government fled to Taiwan. The victory made the world''s most populous country communist and changed the balance of the Cold War.'),
  ('NATO founded', 1949, 'The North Atlantic Treaty was signed in Washington on 4 April 1949 by twelve countries from North America and Western Europe, among them the United States, Canada, Britain and France. Its key clause, Article 5, says that an armed attack on one member is an attack on all, which was meant to deter the Soviet Union after the Berlin blockade of 1948. West Germany joined in 1955, and the Soviet Union answered with the Warsaw Pact. Article 5 was invoked for the first time after the attacks of 11 September 2001, and NATO has since grown to 32 members.'),
  ('Korean War begins', 1950, 'The Korean War began on 25 June 1950, when North Korea, with Soviet backing, invaded South Korea across the 38th parallel. A U.S.-led UN force under General MacArthur landed at Inchon in September and drove north, until China entered the war in October. After a see-saw fight the front settled near the 38th parallel, and an armistice was signed on 27 July 1953, without a peace treaty. About three million people died, many of them civilians, and Korea is still divided.'),
  ('People''s Republic of China enters Korean War', 1950, 'In October 1950 Chinese ''volunteer'' armies crossed the Yalu River in secret and attacked the UN forces that were advancing toward the Chinese border. Mao Zedong feared a hostile American presence next to China, and the first large Chinese attacks drove the UN troops back from the Yalu and out of North Korea in a few weeks. The Chinese intervention turned a UN victory into a long stalemate, and General MacArthur, who wanted to widen the war, was dismissed by President Truman in April 1951. It cost China hundreds of thousands of casualties but made it a major power, and it left China and the United States enemies for two decades.'),
  ('European Coal and Steel Community', 1951, 'The European Coal and Steel Community was created by the Treaty of Paris, signed on 18 April 1951 by France, West Germany, Italy, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg. It was proposed in 1950 by the French foreign minister Robert Schuman, with ideas from Jean Monnet, to put the coal and steel industries of the former enemies under a common authority, so that war between France and Germany would become unthinkable. It was the first of the European Communities and a model of supranational cooperation. It led to the European Economic Community in 1957 and eventually to the European Union.'),
  ('Battle of Dien Bien Phu', 1954, 'From 13 March to 7 May 1954 French forces at Dien Bien Phu, an outpost in a remote valley of northwestern Vietnam, were besieged by the Viet Minh of General Giap. The French had expected to lure the Viet Minh into a battle that their superior firepower would win, but Giap''s men hauled artillery in pieces over the mountains, dug in on the heights above the valley and shelled the garrison and its airstrip. The French surrendered with around 10,000 prisoners. It was a decisive defeat that ended French rule in Indochina, and it led to the Geneva Accords and the division of Vietnam.'),
  ('Vietnam War', 1955, 'The Vietnam War, fought from about 1955 to 1975, pitted communist North Vietnam, backed by the Soviet Union and China, and the Viet Cong guerrillas against South Vietnam and the United States, after the 1954 division of the country. The U.S. sent advisers and then, from 1965, large numbers of combat troops, who reached more than 500,000 by 1968. The Tet Offensive of 1968 and growing protests turned American opinion against the war, and U.S. troops left in 1973, and South Vietnam fell to the North on 30 April 1975. The war killed more than 58,000 Americans and well over a million Vietnamese, and left deep marks on American politics and culture.'),
  ('Suez Crisis', 1956, 'In July 1956 Egypt''s President Nasser nationalized the Suez Canal, which was owned mainly by British and French shareholders. Britain, France and Israel secretly planned a joint attack, and Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October, followed by British and French landings to ''separate the combatants'' and retake the canal. The United States and the Soviet Union both opposed the invasion, and the pressure, including a run on the pound, forced the three countries to withdraw. The crisis humiliated Britain and France, showed that the superpowers were now in charge, and made Nasser a hero in the Arab world.'),
  ('Ghana independence', 1957, 'On 6 March 1957 the British colony of the Gold Coast became independent as Ghana, led by Kwame Nkrumah, the first sub-Saharan colony to win independence in the postwar era. The new name recalled the medieval Ghana Empire, and the black star on the flag became a symbol of African freedom. Nkrumah, who believed in African unity, helped to inspire other independence movements, and in 1960 alone seventeen African colonies became independent. Nkrumah himself was overthrown in a coup in 1966.'),
  ('Treaty of Rome', 1957, 'The Treaty of Rome, signed on 25 March 1957 by France, West Germany, Italy, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg, created the European Economic Community, usually called the Common Market. Its aim was to remove tariffs and barriers so that goods, workers, services and capital could move freely between the six countries, and to create common policies, for example on agriculture. The treaty took effect on 1 January 1958, and the EEC grew to include Britain, Spain and many others. It became the foundation of today''s European Union.'),
  ('Great Leap Forward', 1958, 'The Great Leap Forward, launched by Mao Zedong in 1958, was a campaign to transform China quickly into an industrial power by organizing the peasants into huge people''s communes and having them make steel in backyard furnaces. Local officials, under pressure, reported huge harvests that did not exist, and the state took grain based on those false numbers, while farm work was neglected. The result was the Great Chinese Famine of 1959-1961, in which tens of millions of people starved to death, one of the deadliest disasters in history. Mao lost some of his authority, and he later launched the Cultural Revolution to regain it.'),
  ('Cuban Revolution', 1959, 'The Cuban Revolution was the movement led by Fidel Castro, with his brother Raúl and Che Guevara, that overthrew the dictator Fulgencio Batista, who fled the country on 1 January 1959. Castro''s small guerrilla force had fought in the Sierra Maestra mountains since 1956 and gained the support of many Cubans angry with Batista''s corruption and repression. Castro soon turned Cuba into a communist state allied with the Soviet Union, and nationalized American property, which led to a U.S. embargo. The revolution led to the Bay of Pigs invasion in 1961 and the Cuban Missile Crisis in 1962.'),
  ('Berlin Wall built', 1961, 'On the night of 12-13 August 1961 East Germany sealed the border between East and West Berlin with barbed wire, later replaced by a concrete wall, to stop its citizens fleeing to the West. About 3.5 million East Germans, many of them young and skilled workers, had left since 1949. The Wall stood for 28 years, and at least 140 people were killed trying to cross it. It became the main symbol of the Cold War division of Europe, and its fall on 9 November 1989 led to German reunification.'),
  ('Cuban Missile Crisis', 1962, 'In October 1962 an American U-2 spy plane photographed Soviet nuclear missile sites being built in Cuba, and President Kennedy responded with a naval blockade, which he called a quarantine, and a demand that the missiles be removed. For thirteen days the United States and the Soviet Union came closer to nuclear war than at any other time. The crisis ended when Khrushchev agreed to withdraw the missiles, in return for a public American promise not to invade Cuba and a secret agreement to remove U.S. missiles from Turkey. It led to a hotline between Washington and Moscow and to the first nuclear test ban treaty in 1963.'),
  ('Cultural Revolution', 1966, 'The Cultural Revolution was launched by Mao Zedong in 1966 to purge supposed capitalist and ''revisionist'' elements from the Communist Party and to regain the power he had lost after the Great Leap Forward. Millions of young Red Guards, waving Mao''s Little Red Book, attacked teachers, officials and intellectuals, and destroyed temples, books and works of art. Schools were closed, millions of people were persecuted or sent to the countryside, and hundreds of thousands died, perhaps many more. It ended only with Mao''s death in 1976, and the Communist Party later called it a catastrophe.'),
  ('Six-Day War', 1967, 'In the Six-Day War of 5-10 June 1967 Israel defeated Egypt, Jordan and Syria. It began after Egypt closed the Straits of Tiran and sent troops into Sinai, when Israel launched air strikes that destroyed most of the Egyptian air force on the ground. Israel captured the Sinai Peninsula and the Gaza Strip, the West Bank and East Jerusalem, and the Golan Heights, which more than tripled the territory it controlled. The war shattered Arab hopes of defeating Israel, created a new wave of Palestinian refugees, and left an occupation that is still at the heart of the conflict.'),
  ('Battle of Hue', 1968, 'On 31 January 1968, at the start of the Tet Offensive, North Vietnamese and Viet Cong troops seized most of Hue, the old imperial capital of Vietnam, and flew their flag over the Citadel. U.S. Marines and South Vietnamese troops needed a month of brutal house-to-house fighting, and heavy bombing and shelling, to retake the city, which was largely destroyed. During the occupation the Communists executed thousands of civilians, many of whom were buried in mass graves. It was one of the bloodiest battles of the war, and with the rest of the Tet Offensive it turned American public opinion against the war.'),
  ('Battle of Khe Sanh', 1968, 'From January to July 1968 a U.S. Marine combat base at Khe Sanh, near the border with Laos in the northwest corner of South Vietnam, was besieged by thousands of North Vietnamese troops. Many Americans feared a new Dien Bien Phu, but the Marines, supplied from the air and supported by massive bombing, held the base, and the siege was lifted in April. The Americans abandoned the base in the summer, and the Communists claimed that as a victory. Historians still debate whether the siege was a real attempt to take the base or a diversion before the Tet Offensive.'),
  ('Prague Spring', 1968, 'The Prague Spring began in January 1968, when the reformer Alexander Dubček became leader of the Czechoslovak Communist Party and promised ''socialism with a human face''. Censorship was eased, citizens gained new freedoms of speech and travel, and plans were made for economic and political reform. The Soviet leaders feared that the movement would spread, and on the night of 20-21 August 1968 hundreds of thousands of Warsaw Pact troops and thousands of tanks invaded Czechoslovakia. Dubček was removed, a hard-line regime returned, and the invasion showed the limits the USSR would accept in Eastern Europe, until the Velvet Revolution of 1989.'),
  ('Battle of Hamburger Hill', 1969, 'In May 1969, during Operation Apache Snow, U.S. paratroopers of the 101st Airborne Division and South Vietnamese troops attacked North Vietnamese positions on Hill 937, in the A Shau Valley. After ten days of assaults up the muddy, jungle-covered slopes in heavy rain, in which about 70 Americans were killed, the hill was taken, and the men called it Hamburger Hill because it ground up soldiers like meat. The U.S. abandoned the hill a few weeks later, which caused an outcry in Congress and in the press over a costly fight with no lasting gain. The controversy helped to push the army toward a less aggressive strategy and the gradual withdrawal of U.S. troops.'),
  ('Moon landing', 1969, 'On 20 July 1969 the Apollo 11 lunar module Eagle landed in the Sea of Tranquility, and Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin became the first humans to walk on the Moon, while Michael Collins stayed in orbit above. They spent about two and a half hours outside, planted the American flag, and brought back about 21 kg of lunar rock and soil. Some 600 million people watched on television, and the mission fulfilled President Kennedy''s 1961 goal of landing a man on the Moon before the end of the decade. It was the high point of the Space Race, and twelve men walked on the Moon before the Apollo program ended in 1972.'),
  ('Bangladesh Liberation War', 1971, 'The Bangladesh Liberation War began in March 1971, after West Pakistan''s military government refused to let the Bengali Awami League, which had won the national election, take power. On the night of 25 March the Pakistani army launched a brutal crackdown in East Pakistan, in which hundreds of thousands of people were killed by most estimates, and about ten million refugees fled to India. India intervened in December, and the Pakistani army in the east surrendered in Dhaka on 16 December, with some 93,000 troops, the largest surrender since the Second World War. East Pakistan became the independent country of Bangladesh.'),
  ('Indo-Pakistani War', 1971, 'The Indo-Pakistani War of 1971 was fought for thirteen days in December, after Pakistan launched air strikes on Indian airfields on 3 December. India, which had been sheltering millions of refugees from the crisis in East Pakistan, attacked on both fronts, and its army and the Bengali Mukti Bahini guerrillas advanced quickly on Dhaka. Pakistan''s forces in the east surrendered on 16 December, and a ceasefire followed in the west. The war led to the creation of Bangladesh, and left India as the dominant power in South Asia.'),
  ('Chile coup', 1973, 'On 11 September 1973 the Chilean armed forces, led by General Augusto Pinochet, overthrew the elected socialist president Salvador Allende, bombing the presidential palace, La Moneda, where Allende died during the attack, in what investigators have concluded was suicide. The United States, which was hostile to Allende''s government, had worked to undermine it. Pinochet ruled as dictator until 1990, and thousands of people were killed or made to disappear, and tens of thousands were tortured. His government also introduced free-market reforms, and Chile returned to democracy after a referendum in 1988.'),
  ('Yom Kippur War', 1973, 'The Yom Kippur War began on 6 October 1973, when Egypt and Syria launched a surprise attack on Israel on the holiest day of the Jewish year. The Egyptian army crossed the Suez Canal and broke through Israel''s defensive line, while the Syrians attacked on the Golan Heights, and Israel, taken off guard, suffered heavy early losses before it counterattacked and crossed the canal. The war brought the United States and the Soviet Union close to confrontation, and the Arab oil embargo that followed caused a world oil crisis. It restored Egyptian pride and led to the peace talks that ended in the Egypt-Israel peace treaty of 1979.'),
  ('Fall of Saigon', 1975, 'On 30 April 1975 North Vietnamese tanks crashed through the gates of the presidential palace in Saigon, and South Vietnam''s last president, Duong Van Minh, surrendered. Hours before, the United States had evacuated its embassy by helicopter, and the pictures of people climbing to the rooftop helicopters became a symbol of America''s defeat. The fall of Saigon ended the Vietnam War, and Vietnam was reunified under communist rule in 1976. Hundreds of thousands of people fled in the following years as ''boat people'', and many others were sent to re-education camps.'),
  ('Fall of Saigon / European détente era', 1975, '1975 was a turning point of the Cold War. In Asia, the fall of Saigon on 30 April completed the communist victory in Vietnam, and communists also took power in Cambodia and Laos, which was a blow to American prestige. In Europe the same year saw the high point of détente, when 35 states, among them the United States, the Soviet Union and Canada, signed the Helsinki Final Act on 1 August, accepting Europe''s postwar borders and promising to respect human rights. Dissidents in the Soviet bloc later used those promises to challenge their governments, and the Apollo-Soyuz space link-up in July became a symbol of cooperation.'),
  ('Lebanese Civil War', 1975, 'The Lebanese Civil War began in April 1975, with fighting in Beirut between Christian Phalangist militiamen and Palestinian armed groups, and grew into a complex war between many Lebanese factions, Christian, Muslim and leftist, in which Syria and Israel also intervened. Beirut was split along the ''Green Line'', and the war killed an estimated 120,000 people and displaced nearly a million. It ended in 1990 under the Taif Agreement, which shared political power more equally between Christians and Muslims. The war left Syrian troops in Lebanon until 2005 and led to the rise of Hezbollah.'),
  ('Deng Xiaoping reforms', 1978, 'In December 1978 the Communist Party''s Central Committee, under Deng Xiaoping, who had become China''s leading figure after Mao''s death, adopted a policy of ''reform and opening up''. Farming was returned from the communes to peasant families, special economic zones such as Shenzhen were opened to foreign investment, and private business was allowed again. China''s economy grew by about ten per cent a year for decades, lifting hundreds of millions of people out of poverty, and it became the world''s second-largest economy. The Party, however, kept its monopoly on political power.'),
  ('Iranian Revolution', 1979, 'The Iranian Revolution of 1978-1979 overthrew Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi, whose autocratic, Westernizing and U.S.-backed rule and secret police had angered many Iranians. Huge protests by Islamists, leftists, merchants and students forced the Shah to leave in January 1979, and the exiled Ayatollah Khomeini returned in triumph on 1 February. A referendum in April replaced the monarchy with an Islamic Republic, and the seizure of the U.S. embassy in November, with 52 hostages held for 444 days, set Iran and the United States against each other. The revolution transformed the Middle East and shocked world oil markets.'),
  ('Soviet invasion of Afghanistan', 1979, 'On 24-27 December 1979 Soviet airborne and ground forces entered Afghanistan, killed the president Hafizullah Amin and installed Babrak Karmal, to prop up the communist government against Islamic rebels. The Soviet army fought the mujahideen for nearly ten years, in a war in which about 15,000 Soviet soldiers and around a million Afghans were killed. The United States, Pakistan and Saudi Arabia armed the rebels, and many countries boycotted the 1980 Moscow Olympics. The Soviets withdrew in 1989, and the long war weakened the USSR and left Afghanistan in chaos, from which the Taliban and al-Qaeda emerged.'),
  ('Iran-Iraq War', 1980, 'The Iran-Iraq War began on 22 September 1980, when Saddam Hussein''s Iraq invaded Iran, hoping to profit from the chaos after the Islamic Revolution and to seize the Shatt al-Arab waterway. Iran pushed the Iraqis back by 1982, and the war settled into years of trench fighting like the First World War, with human-wave attacks, attacks on shipping and cities, and Iraqi use of chemical weapons against Iranian troops and Kurdish civilians. It ended in a ceasefire in August 1988 with no change in the borders. Several hundred thousand people were killed, perhaps as many as a million.'),
  ('Chernobyl Disaster', 1986, 'On 26 April 1986 reactor number 4 of the Chernobyl nuclear power plant, in Soviet Ukraine, exploded during a safety test, because of a flawed reactor design and the mistakes of the operators. The fire sent a huge cloud of radioactive material over Ukraine, Belarus and much of Europe, and dozens of plant workers and firefighters died in the following weeks. More than 100,000 people were evacuated from the area, including the town of Pripyat, and a large zone around the plant is still closed. The Soviet authorities were slow to admit the scale of the accident, and it damaged trust in the Soviet system.'),
  ('Doi Moi reforms', 1986, 'In December 1986 the Vietnamese Communist Party, at its Sixth Congress, launched Doi Moi, which means ''renovation'', to move the country from a centrally planned economy toward a ''socialist-oriented market economy''. Farm families were given control of their land, private firms were allowed, and foreign investment and trade were welcomed. The reforms followed a deep economic crisis of food shortages and high inflation. Vietnam grew quickly, became a major exporter and cut poverty sharply, while the Communist Party kept political control.'),
  ('Fall of Berlin Wall', 1989, 'On 9 November 1989 East Germany''s government announced new travel rules, and its spokesman Günter Schabowski, by mistake, said that they took effect at once. Thousands of East Berliners went to the border crossings, and the overwhelmed guards opened the gates that night, so that crowds crossed freely into West Berlin and climbed on the Wall in celebration. The Wall, built in 1961, had divided the city for 28 years. Its fall, during a wave of revolutions across Eastern Europe, led to German reunification in 1990 and became the symbol of the end of the Cold War.'),
  ('Tiananmen protests', 1989, 'The Tiananmen Square protests of April-June 1989 began after the death of the reformist leader Hu Yaobang, when students gathered in the centre of Beijing, and were joined by workers and others, to demand political reform, a free press and an end to corruption. Hunger strikes and huge crowds followed, with similar protests in many Chinese cities, and martial law was declared in May. On the night of 3-4 June the army cleared the square and the streets leading to it with tanks and live fire, killing many people, with estimates ranging from several hundred to thousands. The protests were crushed, and the events are still censored in China.'),
  ('German reunification', 1990, 'On 3 October 1990 East Germany joined the Federal Republic of Germany, ending the division of Germany that had lasted since 1949. The way had been opened by the fall of the Berlin Wall in 1989, East Germany''s first free elections in March 1990, and a monetary union in July. The ''Two Plus Four'' treaty, signed with the four wartime Allies, gave the united Germany full sovereignty and allowed it to stay in NATO, which Gorbachev accepted. Chancellor Helmut Kohl led the process, and 3 October is now Germany''s national day.'),
  ('Gulf War', 1990, 'On 2 August 1990 Saddam Hussein''s Iraq invaded and occupied neighbouring Kuwait, and the UN Security Council demanded its withdrawal and imposed sanctions. A coalition of 42 countries led by the United States, including several Arab states, built up forces in Saudi Arabia. In January 1991 a five-week air campaign began, followed by a ground offensive that liberated Kuwait in only 100 hours, with few coalition casualties. Saddam stayed in power, and the war showed the power of modern precision weapons.'),
  ('Dissolution of Soviet Union', 1991, 'The Soviet Union was dissolved on 26 December 1991, after a failed coup by hard-liners against President Gorbachev in August had weakened the central government and strengthened Boris Yeltsin. Ukraine voted overwhelmingly for independence in December, and the leaders of Russia, Ukraine and Belarus declared that the USSR had ceased to exist. Gorbachev resigned on 25 December, and the Soviet flag was lowered over the Kremlin. The union broke up into fifteen independent states, which ended the Cold War and left Russia as its main successor.'),
  ('Maastricht Treaty', 1992, 'The Maastricht Treaty, signed on 7 February 1992 by the twelve members of the European Communities and in force from 1993, created the European Union. It introduced European citizenship, joint policies on foreign affairs and on justice and home affairs, and set strict economic conditions and a timetable for a single currency, the euro, which was launched in 1999. Britain and Denmark won exemptions on some points. The treaty marked the move from an economic community to a political union.'),
  ('Oslo Accords', 1993, 'The Oslo Accords were agreements between Israel and the Palestine Liberation Organization, reached in secret talks in Norway and signed in Washington on 13 September 1993, when Yitzhak Rabin and Yasser Arafat shook hands on the White House lawn. Israel and the PLO recognized each other, and the Palestinian Authority was created to govern parts of the West Bank and Gaza during a five-year interim period before talks on the final issues, such as Jerusalem, borders and refugees. A second agreement in 1995 divided the West Bank into zones. Rabin was assassinated by a Jewish extremist in 1995, and the peace process later broke down, without reaching a final settlement.'),
  ('End of apartheid', 1994, 'In April 1994 South Africa held its first election in which people of all races could vote, ending the apartheid system of white-minority rule and racial segregation that had been in force since 1948. The African National Congress won, and Nelson Mandela, who had spent 27 years in prison, became president on 10 May, leading a government of national unity. The change was negotiated between 1990 and 1993 by Mandela and President F. W. de Klerk, who shared the Nobel Peace Prize. South Africa''s peaceful transition, helped by the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, became a model for settling deep conflicts.'),
  ('Hong Kong handover', 1997, 'At midnight on 1 July 1997 Hong Kong was handed over by Britain to China, ending 156 years of British rule that had begun with the capture of the island in 1841. Under the 1984 Sino-British Joint Declaration it became a Special Administrative Region under the principle of ''one country, two systems'', promised its own legal system and freedoms for fifty years. It was Britain''s last major colony. Critics say that China''s national security law of 2020 has since reduced the city''s autonomy.'),
  ('Macau handover', 1999, 'On 20 December 1999 Macau was handed over by Portugal to China, ending 442 years of Portuguese presence that had begun in 1557, and the last European colony in Asia. As with Hong Kong, it became a Special Administrative Region under the principle of ''one country, two systems'', with a high degree of autonomy for fifty years. The handover followed the Sino-Portuguese Joint Declaration of 1987. Macau''s economy later boomed on casino gambling, and by 2006 its gambling revenues had overtaken those of Las Vegas.'),
  ('China joins WTO', 2001, 'China became a member of the World Trade Organization on 11 December 2001, after fifteen years of negotiations, in which it agreed to lower tariffs, open many markets and reform its trade rules. Membership gave Chinese exporters secure access to the world''s markets and attracted huge foreign investment, and China became known as the world''s factory and the largest exporter. The boom lifted hundreds of millions of Chinese out of poverty. It also caused job losses in manufacturing in the United States and Europe and led to many trade disputes.'),
  ('September 11 attacks', 2001, 'On 11 September 2001 nineteen al-Qaeda hijackers seized four airliners and crashed two of them into the Twin Towers of the World Trade Center in New York, and a third into the Pentagon, while the fourth crashed in a field in Pennsylvania after passengers tried to retake it. Almost 3,000 people were killed, in the deadliest terrorist attack in history. The United States responded with the ''war on terror'', invading Afghanistan in October to overthrow the Taliban, who sheltered Osama bin Laden. The attacks led to the Iraq War, to new security measures around the world, and changed global politics.'),
  ('U.S.-led invasion of Iraq', 2003, 'On 20 March 2003 a coalition led by the United States, with Britain, Australia and Poland, invaded Iraq to overthrow Saddam Hussein, claiming that he had weapons of mass destruction and links to terrorism, claims that proved false. Baghdad fell on 9 April, after just over three weeks of major combat, and Saddam was captured in December. The invasion, which lacked approval from the UN Security Council and was opposed by France and Germany, was followed by a long insurgency and sectarian violence. It killed hundreds of thousands of Iraqis by many estimates and created conditions in which the Islamic State later arose.'),
  ('EU enlargement eastward', 2004, 'On 1 May 2004 ten countries joined the European Union, Poland, the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Hungary, Slovenia, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Cyprus and Malta, in the biggest enlargement in its history. Eight of them were former communist states of Central and Eastern Europe, so it ended the Cold War division of the continent. The EU grew from 15 to 25 members, and Bulgaria and Romania followed in 2007. The new members gained access to the single market and EU funds, and their workers gained the right to move freely, which led to debates on migration.'),
  ('Global financial crisis in Europe', 2008, 'The global financial crisis began in the United States, where the collapse of the housing bubble and of risky mortgage lending put big banks in trouble, and it exploded after the investment bank Lehman Brothers failed on 15 September 2008. Credit froze around the world, and governments spent huge sums on rescuing banks and on stimulus. In Europe the crisis hit banks and public finances, and from 2010 it developed into a eurozone debt crisis, with bailouts and harsh austerity in Greece, Ireland, Portugal and other countries. It caused the Great Recession, brought tighter financial rules, and fueled political anger and populist movements.'),
  ('Arab Spring begins', 2010, 'The Arab Spring began on 17 December 2010 in Tunisia, when a young street vendor, Mohamed Bouazizi, set himself on fire in protest at police harassment and corruption. Mass protests spread across the country and forced President Ben Ali to flee on 14 January 2011, and similar protests then swept the Arab world. In Egypt Hosni Mubarak was forced from power, in Libya Muammar Gaddafi was overthrown and killed, and in Syria and Yemen the unrest turned into civil war. Tunisia alone made a lasting transition to democracy for some years, and elsewhere the results were war or a return to authoritarian rule.'),
  ('Syrian Civil War begins', 2011, 'The Syrian Civil War began in March 2011, when peaceful protests in the town of Daraa, after the arrest and mistreatment of teenagers who had written graffiti against the government, were met with force by President Bashar al-Assad''s regime. The protests grew into an armed rebellion and a complex war among many groups, including the Islamic State, with foreign backers such as Russia and Iran on Assad''s side and Turkey, Gulf states and the West on the side of various rebels. More than half a million people were killed, and over half of Syria''s pre-war population was forced to flee their homes, in the worst refugee crisis of the era. Assad was finally overthrown in December 2024 by a rebel offensive.'),
  ('Rise of ISIS', 2013, 'The Islamic State, or ISIS, grew out of al-Qaeda in Iraq and in 2013, under Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi, expanded into the chaos of the Syrian civil war, taking advantage also of the grievances of Iraq''s Sunni Arabs. In June 2014 its fighters captured the Iraqi city of Mosul, and Baghdadi declared a ''caliphate'' over large areas of Iraq and Syria. The group became known for mass killings, the enslavement of Yazidi women and the filmed murder of captives, and it inspired terrorist attacks abroad. A U.S.-led coalition and local forces drove it out of its last territory by 2019.'),
  ('Iran nuclear deal', 2015, 'The Iran nuclear deal, or JCPOA, was signed in Vienna on 14 July 2015 by Iran and the United States, Britain, France, Russia, China and Germany. Iran agreed to limit its uranium enrichment, sharply reduce its stock of enriched uranium and accept international inspections, in return for the lifting of sanctions that had damaged its economy. Supporters saw it as a way to block Iran from building a nuclear bomb, while critics, among them Israel and the Gulf states, thought it too weak and temporary. In 2018 President Trump withdrew the United States and reimposed sanctions, and Iran later went beyond the limits of the deal.'),
  ('Brexit referendum', 2016, 'On 23 June 2016 the United Kingdom voted by 51.9 per cent to 48.1 per cent to leave the European Union, in a referendum called by Prime Minister David Cameron, who then resigned. England and Wales voted to leave, while Scotland and Northern Ireland voted to remain. After years of difficult negotiations the UK left on 31 January 2020, the first member state ever to leave the EU. Brexit reshaped Britain''s trade and politics and raised hard questions about Northern Ireland''s border and Scotland''s place in the union.'),
  ('Abraham Accords', 2020, 'The Abraham Accords were agreements to normalize relations between Israel and Arab states, announced in August 2020 with the United Arab Emirates and signed at the White House on 15 September with the UAE and Bahrain, with Morocco and Sudan agreeing later. They were the first such deals since Egypt in 1979 and Jordan in 1994, and they brought trade, flights and cooperation. Israel suspended its plans to annex parts of the West Bank, but the Palestinians condemned the accords for ignoring their demands for statehood. Talks on a similar deal with Saudi Arabia were later overshadowed by the war in Gaza.'),
  ('Fall of Kabul', 2021, 'On 15 August 2021 the Taliban entered Kabul as the Afghan government collapsed, and President Ashraf Ghani fled the country. Their offensive had begun in May, as U.S. and NATO forces completed the withdrawal agreed with the Taliban in 2020, and the Afghan army, which the United States had trained and funded for twenty years, largely dissolved within days. The evacuation of foreigners and Afghans from Kabul airport was chaotic, and a suicide bombing there killed 13 U.S. service members and many Afghans. The Taliban restored the Islamic Emirate, and have imposed severe limits on women''s rights and girls'' education.'),
  ('Israel-Gaza war begins', 2023, 'The Gaza war began on 7 October 2023, when Hamas-led fighters attacked southern Israel, killing about 1,200 people, most of them civilians, and taking around 250 hostages. Israel responded with a large air and ground campaign in the Gaza Strip, which, according to the Gaza health authorities, killed tens of thousands of Palestinians, displaced most of the population and caused a severe humanitarian crisis. The fighting spread tensions to Lebanon, Yemen and Iran, and drew widespread international diplomatic and legal attention. It became the most serious Israeli-Palestinian conflict in decades.');
